WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:04.922
Welcome. Equity becomes practical when we look closely at

2
00:00:04.944 --> 00:00:08.456
a decision: what it is meant to accomplish, who

3
00:00:08.478 --> 00:00:11.678
may experience it, and what could make that experience

4
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.278
more or less fair. You do not have to

5
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.389
solve every problem at once. You can begin with

6
00:00:17.411 --> 00:00:19.344
one question in your own work.

7
00:00:20.157 --> 00:00:24.179
This learning is self-directed. You can pause, revisit a

8
00:00:24.201 --> 00:00:27.157
section and use the activities at your own pace.

9
00:00:27.912 --> 00:00:30.379
You may work with the continuing example or choose

10
00:00:30.401 --> 00:00:33.268
a non-sensitive piece of your own work. Participation in

11
00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:37.290
this learning path is voluntary. Follow the policies, required

12
00:00:37.312 --> 00:00:40.046
analyses and training that apply to your work separately.

13
00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:45.046
The lessons are a companion to the existing Equity

14
00:00:45.068 --> 00:00:49.312
Analysis Toolkit and other One DSD resources. They help

15
00:00:49.334 --> 00:00:52.179
you prepare and practice. They do not replace the

16
00:00:52.201 --> 00:00:55.446
official tool, required review, or the judgment of the

17
00:00:55.468 --> 00:00:58.023
people and offices responsible for a decision.

18
00:00:59.186 --> 00:01:03.341
Consider an appointment reminder. A team is thinking about

19
00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:07.674
replacing mailed reminders with text messages only. The team

20
00:01:07.697 --> 00:01:09.919
hopes a text might be a useful way to

21
00:01:09.941 --> 00:01:13.119
reach people. No change has been approved, and the

22
00:01:13.141 --> 00:01:16.052
team has not yet established which options people can

23
00:01:16.074 --> 00:01:17.030
use or prefer.

24
00:01:17.681 --> 00:01:20.548
At first, this can look like a question about

25
00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.681
how to send a message. For the person receiving

26
00:01:23.703 --> 00:01:27.148
it, the question is larger: Will I receive the

27
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:30.170
reminder in a way I can use? Will I

28
00:01:30.192 --> 00:01:33.503
understand it? Can I act on it or ask

29
00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:37.792
for help? Those questions bring the intended experience into

30
00:01:37.814 --> 00:01:38.214
view.

31
00:01:38.818 --> 00:01:41.729
There may be benefits to texting. There may also

32
00:01:41.751 --> 00:01:46.329
be concerns about access, language, shared phones, privacy, disability-related

33
00:01:46.351 --> 00:01:50.307
needs, or a change in contact information. Treat these

34
00:01:50.329 --> 00:01:53.484
as questions to investigate, not assumptions about any person

35
00:01:53.507 --> 00:01:54.284
or community.

36
00:01:55.407 --> 00:01:58.851
A clear decision statement names the choice under consideration

37
00:01:58.873 --> 00:02:02.007
and the purpose it is supposed to serve. For

38
00:02:02.029 --> 00:02:04.984
this example, the question is whether and how to

39
00:02:05.007 --> 00:02:08.673
offer appointment reminders, including whether a text-only approach should

40
00:02:08.696 --> 00:02:10.140
replace mailed reminders.

41
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.996
Notice what that statement does not decide. It does

42
00:02:14.018 --> 00:02:18.262
not change eligibility, appointment requirements or an individual's services.

43
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.107
It also does not assume that a new communication

44
00:02:21.129 --> 00:02:24.707
method has already been authorized. Those boundaries help the

45
00:02:24.729 --> 00:02:26.462
right people review the right question.

46
00:02:27.209 --> 00:02:30.298
If your work involves a form, a meeting process,

47
00:02:30.631 --> 00:02:33.898
a contract, a hiring practice or a service handoff,

48
00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.853
the same habit applies. State what may change, what

49
00:02:37.876 --> 00:02:40.609
is staying outside the proposal, and who has authority

50
00:02:40.631 --> 00:02:44.164
to decide. If a boundary is uncertain, write the

51
00:02:44.187 --> 00:02:45.942
question down rather than guessing.

52
00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:50.843
The people affected may include those receiving reminders, staff

53
00:02:50.866 --> 00:02:54.599
who prepare them, people who provide communication support, and

54
00:02:54.621 --> 00:02:58.021
partners responsible for the service. Their roles and perspectives

55
00:02:58.043 --> 00:03:01.443
differ. A convenient process for one person may require

56
00:03:01.466 --> 00:03:02.621
more effort from another.

57
00:03:03.254 --> 00:03:06.588
Equity does not mean assuming that a particular group

58
00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:09.721
will respond in a particular way. Ask what could

59
00:03:09.743 --> 00:03:12.988
affect access and whose experience would help you understand

60
00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:16.432
it. Be attentive to differences without turning a person's

61
00:03:16.454 --> 00:03:18.943
identity into a prediction about their needs.

62
00:03:19.684 --> 00:03:22.418
You may not have direct contact with the people

63
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:26.262
affected. Your contribution might be to examine the proposal,

64
00:03:26.507 --> 00:03:30.551
notice a missing question, consult the appropriate colleagues, or

65
00:03:30.573 --> 00:03:34.396
help bring existing information into the review. Good work

66
00:03:34.418 --> 00:03:36.773
begins within your role and makes room for the

67
00:03:36.796 --> 00:03:37.596
knowledge of others.

68
00:03:38.789 --> 00:03:41.811
In the activity below, consider how to frame the

69
00:03:41.833 --> 00:03:45.389
proposed reminder change before anyone settles on a method.

70
00:03:46.144 --> 00:03:48.811
Choose the response you think creates the best starting

71
00:03:48.833 --> 00:03:52.567
point. After you choose, read the feedback and compare

72
00:03:52.589 --> 00:03:55.611
the reasoning. You can change your answer and try

73
00:03:55.633 --> 00:03:56.033
again.

74
00:03:56.644 --> 00:04:00.911
There is no need to enter personal information. The

75
00:04:00.933 --> 00:04:03.289
activity is for practice; it does not make a

76
00:04:03.311 --> 00:04:06.778
decision for an actual person or approve a change.

77
00:04:08.017 --> 00:04:10.861
If you would like, write a brief note about

78
00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:14.706
a decision you can examine safely. Name its purpose,

79
00:04:15.372 --> 00:04:18.061
the choice that is still open, the people who

80
00:04:18.083 --> 00:04:20.994
may be affected and a question about your authority

81
00:04:21.017 --> 00:04:24.306
or next review. You can also stay with the

82
00:04:24.328 --> 00:04:26.617
reminder example throughout the series.

83
00:04:27.369 --> 00:04:32.080
Keep names, case details, health information, personnel records and

84
00:04:32.102 --> 00:04:35.902
other confidential information out of learning notes. Follow the

85
00:04:35.924 --> 00:04:38.947
instructions beside any tool you use to understand whether

86
00:04:38.969 --> 00:04:41.813
its notes are saved, downloaded or kept only for

87
00:04:41.836 --> 00:04:42.569
the current visit.

88
00:04:43.158 --> 00:04:46.713
Next, we will consider how much review the decision

89
00:04:46.736 --> 00:04:50.647
needs. A useful first question is not simply how

90
00:04:50.669 --> 00:04:53.580
quickly a decision can be made. It is what

91
00:04:53.602 --> 00:04:56.380
the work requires and what could happen if an

92
00:04:56.402 --> 00:04:58.202
important perspective is missed.
